language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ flow_free_game: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Connect matching colours with pipes to create a flow: pair up all the colours and cover the entire board, so that no pipes cross or overlap. The board has rows and columns; cells with value 0 are empty and need to be filled with a colour from 1 to 5, while numbered cells keep their colour. Cells are connected horizontally and vertically: a numbered cell has exactly one neighbour of its own colour, and a filled cell has exactly two. Give the colour of every cell of the board as an integer from 1 to 5 (B).
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `flow_free_game.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/flow_free_game.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `flow_free_game_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py flow_free_game.json
$   savilerow model.eprime flow_free_game_1.param -run-solver

$ board_dim1, board_dim2: the number of rows and of columns of the board (derived from the data)
given board_dim1, board_dim2 : int
$ the number of colours (colours are numbered 1 to 5)
letting colours = 5
$ board[i,j]: the numbered colour of cell (i,j), or 0 for an empty cell; indices 1-based
given board : matrix indexed by [int(1..board_dim1), int(1..board_dim2)] of int(0..colours)

$ B[i,j]: the colour of cell (i,j) in the solution
find B : matrix indexed by [int(1..board_dim1), int(1..board_dim2)] of int(1..colours)
$ nbr[i,j]: the number of neighbours of cell (i,j), above, below, left or right, that have the same colour as it (auxiliary)
find nbr : matrix indexed by [int(1..board_dim1), int(1..board_dim2)] of int(0..4)

branching on [B]

such that
$ each numbered cell keeps its colour
forAll i : int(1..board_dim1) . forAll j : int(1..board_dim2) . (board[i,j] != 0 -> B[i,j] = board[i,j]),
$ nbr counts the neighbours of each cell that share its colour
forAll i : int(1..board_dim1) . forAll j : int(1..board_dim2) . nbr[i,j] = (sum a : int(1..board_dim1) . sum b : int(1..board_dim2) . toInt(((a = i) /\ ((b = j + 1) \/ (b = j - 1))) \/ ((b = j) /\ ((a = i + 1) \/ (a = i - 1)))) * toInt(B[a,b] = B[i,j])),
$ a numbered cell has exactly one neighbour of its own colour
forAll i : int(1..board_dim1) . forAll j : int(1..board_dim2) . (board[i,j] != 0 -> nbr[i,j] = 1),
$ a filled cell has exactly two neighbours of its own colour
forAll i : int(1..board_dim1) . forAll j : int(1..board_dim2) . (board[i,j] = 0 -> nbr[i,j] = 2)
